    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Released in 1990, Cure for Sanity finds Pop Will Eat Itself moving beyond the playful sample-pop of their earlier work toward a darker, more forceful blend of electronic dance music, hip-hop, industrial rock, and alternative rock. Processed samples, aggressive grooves, synth-driven textures, and confrontational themes give the album a more serious and occasionally menacing character, from the pounding “Dance of the Mad Bastards” to the sleek electropop of “X Y & Zee” and the ominous “Nightmare at 20,000 Feet.”\n\nThough its heavier atmosphere and slogan-heavy writing divided critics, the album remains an important document of the late-1980s and early-1990s crossover between indie rock, rave culture, and industrial music. The 2026 Music On Vinyl reissue presents the original album as a limited two-LP edition on 180-gram purple vinyl, with bonus mixes and alternate versions that highlight the band’s connection to the rapidly developing house, techno, and alternative-dance scenes of the period.",
